<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/frozen-pipe.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/frozen-pipe-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/frozen-pipe.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="683" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.15"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><a href="/property-claim-appraisal/">Services</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">Water &amp; Frozen Pipe Appraisal</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Water Damage Appraisal for Frozen Pipes and Sudden Leaks</h1><p class="lede">A January cold snap can split a pipe in an exterior wall or an empty cabin. By the time the water is found, it has reached subfloors, cabinets and insulation. We value the whole job.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-y" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line-w" href="/schedule-a-consult/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="facts"><div><dt>Peak season</dt><dd>December to February</dd></div><div><dt>Common source</dt><dd>Frozen supply lines</dd></div><div><dt>Watch for</dt><dd>Mold &amp; matching</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<h2>Why Idaho water claims are so often frozen-pipe claims</h2>
<p>Sub-zero nights are routine in eastern Idaho, the central mountains and the Panhandle, and the Treasure Valley gets its share of hard freezes. Pipes in crawlspaces, garage walls and north-facing exterior walls are vulnerable, especially in homes left empty over the holidays or seasonal cabins near McCall, Island Park and Sandpoint. When a frozen line thaws and lets go, it can run for hours.</p>
<h2>What gets missed in water damage estimates</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Drying scope</strong> that stops at visible wet drywall and ignores wet insulation, subfloor and cabinet bases.</li>
<li><strong>Flooring continuity.</strong> Hardwood or LVP that runs through several rooms can’t be patched in one.</li>
<li><strong>Cabinet matching</strong> when lower boxes are ruined and uppers can’t be matched.</li>
<li><strong>Contents</strong> priced at garage-sale values.</li>
<li><strong>Mold sub-limits</strong> applied where the growth is a direct result of a covered leak, which depends on the policy wording.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Coverage questions come first</h2>
<p>Water claims are where policy wording matters most. Many forms cover sudden and accidental discharge but exclude long-term seepage, and many restrict freezing losses in vacant or unheated buildings unless heat was maintained or the water was shut off. Flooding from surface water or rising rivers is excluded on standard homeowners forms and needs flood insurance. We tell you plainly whether your issue is about coverage (an attorney question) or amount (an appraisal question).</p>
<h2>How we value the loss</h2>
<p>We review moisture logs, photos and the mitigation company’s invoice, inspect what remains, and price a complete rebuild: demolition, drying, materials, labor and the finish work needed for a uniform result.</p>

<h2>Questions about water damage appraisal</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>My cabin pipes froze while it was empty. Is that covered?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It depends on the policy’s freezing and vacancy conditions. Many require that heat be maintained or that the water be shut off and lines drained. Read those conditions carefully before assuming coverage either way.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is an ice-dam leak a water claim or a roof claim?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Usually it’s handled as interior water damage from a weather event. Some policies treat it differently, and roof repairs may be scoped separately.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can the mitigation company’s bill be appraised?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>If the insurer disputes the reasonable cost of mitigation within a covered loss, that amount can be part of the appraisal.</p></div></details></div>
